articles.statement:
The dichromatic number of a simple graph $G$ is the least natural
number $k$ such that $G$ is dichromatically $k$-colourable, taken in $\mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}$;
it is $\infty$ if $G$ is dichromatically $k$-colourable for no natural number $k$.

Let $V$ be a finite set and $c : V \times V \to \mathbb{R}$ a capacity
function. The capacity of a subset $S \subseteq V$ is the total capacity of the arcs leaving $S$,
$\sum_{u \in S} \sum_{v \in V \setminus S} c(u, v)$.

The formula `cornerDiff S = 4 * eulerChar S` fails in general (two diagonally touching cells);
 see `StatMech.Onsager.CellGaussBonnetGlobal.cornerDiff_eq` for the correct formula with a pinch
 term.
